1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,100
Pray. Amen.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:56,680
Well, good morning, church.

3
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:59,720
Thank you all for braving the weather.

4
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:01,800
So glad that you have joined us.

5
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:03,440
If we haven't met yet, my name is Ryan.

6
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:05,180
I have the privilege of being one of the pastors here.

7
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:08,220
The glasses are a new thing, in case you're wondering.

8
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:13,740
And I'm kind of in between because they're supposed to be for, like, nights and light.

9
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,960
Not really good close up.

10
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:19,320
So my theory is, because it's relatively dark in here with a lot of lights,

11
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:21,480
I should be able to see some of you a lot better.

12
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:23,980
I'm not sure if I'm going to be able to read my notes.

13
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:27,980
So if I make long, awkward eye contact with some of you back further,

14
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:31,820
it's because I can actually see you for the first time ever.

15
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:37,160
And if I stumble over my notes, it's because I'm sacrificing the ability to read them

16
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:41,640
in order to make long, awkward eye contact with you all in the back row.

17
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:44,080
So we are so glad that you are here.

18
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:45,360
We are continuing.

19
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:46,100
Oh, no.

20
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:47,000
Save that for later.

21
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:47,420
All right.

22
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:51,300
That's long, awkward eye contact.

23
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:54,620
We are continuing our series on Advent.

24
00:01:55,620 --> 00:02:00,960
And we've said so far that at Advent, the focus of Advent is to look back at the ways in which

25
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:04,580
God has been faithful to his promises in the past through the birth of Jesus.

26
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:12,120
To think about how we can participate in the promises of God in the present and to look forward with

27
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:14,900
anticipation to the promises of God in the future.

28
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:20,380
We light candles representing certain elements of Christ's character, his nature that he has

29
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:22,160
brought to us through the first Advent.

30
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:30,340
And we know that the light from the candles are a flicker, a dimly lit candle showing us that

31
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:31,540
it's partially come.

32
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:34,300
We're in this already not yet stage of the kingdom of God.

33
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:39,120
We can see hope, love, joy, and peace and dimness.

34
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:43,440
And yet one day the lights will flip on when Jesus returns and all of it will be revealed

35
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:45,220
perfectly and clearly.

36
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:47,700
We start it with hope.

37
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:50,840
I got a new lighter that's like electric.

38
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:52,340
So we're going to see how this goes.

39
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:56,260
I tested it before we got up here, but I'm always nervous.

40
00:02:56,260 --> 00:02:57,240
Hope.

41
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:03,000
We've said that hope is the confident expectation and intentional participation in the promises

42
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:03,480
of God.

43
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:09,320
Not only do we expect God's promises to come true, but we do so with participation, knowing

44
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:14,420
that if we step out in obedience and in faith, that God will use that obedience to do his kingdom

45
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:16,840
work on earth as it is in heaven.

46
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:22,020
Last week we demonstrated the idea of love through hosting Watts.

47
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:26,640
And I just want to pause right now and thank all of you that did anything to make Watts happen.

48
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:40,320
First and foremost, Mike Gad, who led the whole thing with confidence and with just humility

49
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:43,960
and so thankful for all of you that participated in that.

50
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:49,940
And we said that biblical love is most clearly demonstrated through sacrificial presence.

51
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:56,160
And that nothing is seen more clearly than through the incarnation of Jesus.

52
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:57,640
Well, it worked.

53
00:03:57,640 --> 00:03:58,220
Yes.

54
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:03,480
Today we are going to turn our eyes to joy and I will light that candle in just a few minutes.

55
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:04,440
Let's pray.

56
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:06,080
We'll get started.

57
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:11,680
Father, in heaven, we need you.

58
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:17,440
We're so thankful that your love extends to the highest heavens.

59
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:26,660
God, that when we might be in family situations or in life circumstances where we don't feel very loved or we don't feel like we are extending love in the

60
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:34,020
ways that we should, we remind ourselves, God, that you love us so much that you took on human flesh.

61
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:43,220
God, we give you thanks for that and we invite your presence into this room to teach us, to correct us, to challenge us,

62
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:50,400
to move us to obedience, to kingdom living for your glory, for our good and for the good of our neighbors.

63
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:52,240
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

64
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:53,140
Amen.

65
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:58,440
If you want to turn in your Bibles to Luke chapter 2, we're going to get there in just a few minutes.

66
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:08,740
A while back, my friends and I, we got in the habit of creating alliterated nicknames for one another whenever somebody was being outrageous or ridiculous.

67
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:10,820
It was a fun time.

68
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,380
We enjoyed it a lot.

69
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:17,400
You know, some things like Nosy Nelly, Chatty Cathy, Complaining Karen.

70
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,020
Anybody have heard some of these nicknames?

71
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:26,460
Whenever somebody is being ridiculous or obnoxious, we would love to kind of come up with those nicknames.

72
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:31,940
I'm sure you can all relate to someone in your life that takes an emotion to the absolute extreme.

73
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:35,180
Sometimes it can be difficult.

74
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:41,620
One of my favorite comedic examples of this is the famous Saturday Night Live skit, Miss Debbie Downer.

75
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:42,040
Anybody?

76
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:43,900
Wah, wah.

77
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:51,580
Debbie was always looking for opportunities to tell you about the bad news that she recently heard.

78
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:57,740
Whether or not it fit the context of the room or not, she wanted to tell you about the bad news that she heard.

79
00:05:57,740 --> 00:05:59,520
She was Debbie, the downer.

80
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:09,900
We've probably all been in relationships with someone like this that tends to only focus on the negative and bring out all of the negative experiences of their life.

81
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:11,580
It could be a real drain.

82
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:12,060
Right?

83
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:22,020
Like, throughout the skits, I actually watched a couple over Thanksgiving, and all of the friends around the table find ways to remove themselves from Debbie's presence.

84
00:06:22,020 --> 00:06:27,300
Because it can be a lot when somebody only focuses on the negative experiences of life.

85
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:30,320
We've probably all been in that situation.

86
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:36,000
But we've probably also encountered, especially if you grew up in church, the opposite approach.

87
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,160
Let's call him fake Frank.

88
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:39,860
You know the type?

89
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:50,820
The person that seems to ignore or totally explain away all negative emotions in order to put on a happy face that everyone can see through except for him.

90
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:55,420
We've got Debbie Downer on one hand and fake Frank on the other.

91
00:06:55,420 --> 00:07:05,620
And I feel like Christmas time, holiday seasons, more than any other season, they tempt even the most rational of us to swing the pendulum to one of the extremes.

92
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:12,260
Holidays tend to bring about, they tend to strengthen emotions inside of us.

93
00:07:12,260 --> 00:07:20,760
And some of us that are usually rational, we might be tempted to swing to a very negative level of emotions around the holidays.

94
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:23,080
Challenge, difficult, sad.

95
00:07:23,740 --> 00:07:25,660
Or we might be the opposite.

96
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:26,720
We might swing the pendulum.

97
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:36,020
We have a lot of negative things going on in our lives, but we're just going to ignore them and suppress them, at least for the next couple of weeks, to put on our fake, holly, jolly face.

98
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:40,540
But what if there's a third option?

99
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:42,840
A more balanced approach.

100
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:48,140
What if we can sit honestly with the sorrows and disappointments of our life?

101
00:07:48,940 --> 00:07:59,720
If we can be honest about them this holiday season and still experience a deep sense of inner happiness that isn't dependent on the external circumstances of our life.

102
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:00,600
We can put Debbie down.

103
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:05,800
Yeah, I just realized that.

104
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:06,920
I caught her out of the corner of my eye.

105
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:08,060
I was like, wow, wow.

106
00:08:08,060 --> 00:08:10,640
What if there's a third option?

107
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:16,220
An option that doesn't deny the difficult negative circumstances of our lives,

108
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:20,960
and yet still finds an inner sense of joy in the midst of the difficulty.

109
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:23,800
This is what biblical joy is all about.

110
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:28,920
Joy is an internal sense of gratitude and contentment, no matter our external circumstances.

111
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:34,740
It isn't blown away by the storms of life, but it is deeply rooted in the grace of God.

112
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:36,760
It also isn't fake.

113
00:08:36,760 --> 00:08:39,940
It doesn't deny the external circumstances or try and explain them away.

114
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:48,720
It tells the truth about the difficulty of living in a broken world and yet still holds on to gratitude and contentment in the midst of difficulty.

115
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:50,300
I heard one pastor say it this way.

116
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:57,220
She said, happiness is like a candle that flickers in the wind and joy is like a lighthouse steadfast in the storm.

117
00:08:57,220 --> 00:09:00,180
That'll preach, right?

118
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:02,220
That will preach.

119
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:04,680
Paul says it this way in 2 Corinthians 6.

120
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:08,660
He says, we live close to death, but we are still alive.

121
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:11,200
We have been beaten, but we have not been killed.

122
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:15,520
Our hearts ache, but we always have joy.

123
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:21,060
So, this morning we light the candle of joy.

124
00:09:22,460 --> 00:09:26,040
And I invite you to think about this third option.

125
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:33,320
How can we sit in the midst of the difficulty of this broken world and yet have a deep internal sense of joy?

126
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:39,640
How can we say like Paul, our hearts ache, but we have joy?

127
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:44,680
That's the goal for today.

128
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:48,380
We're going to start in Luke chapter 2, starting in verse 8.

129
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:52,640
And we're going to read this surprising announcement of joy from the Christmas story.

130
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:55,920
And many of us are familiar with the story, especially if we watch Charlie Brown.

131
00:09:55,920 --> 00:10:04,260
Hopefully, I don't sound too much like Linus when I read this, but let's not let the familiarity of the story,

132
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:08,800
let the contents of the story drift past us.

133
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:11,080
Luke chapter 2, verse 8 says,

134
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:17,080
That night after Jesus was born, there were shepherds staying in the fields nearby, guarding their flocks of sheep.

135
00:10:17,620 --> 00:10:23,200
Suddenly, an angel of the Lord appeared among them, and the radiance of the Lord's glory surrounded them.

136
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:28,420
And they were, I've got a bad translation, sore afraid, right?

137
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:33,800
He said, I bring you good news that will bring great joy to all people.

138
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:39,900
The Savior, yes, the Messiah, the Lord has been born today in Bethlehem, the city of David.

139
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:42,220
And you will recognize him by this sign.

140
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:47,520
You will find a baby wrapped snugly in strips of cloth and lying in a manger.

141
00:10:47,520 --> 00:10:53,640
Suddenly, the angels was joined by a vast host of others, the armies of heaven, praising God and saying,

142
00:10:53,640 --> 00:11:00,720
Glory to God in the highest heaven and peace on earth to those with whom God is well.

143
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:01,640
Please.

144
00:11:03,180 --> 00:11:09,300
So we're going to spend the majority of our time focusing in on the announcement that the angel is giving to the shepherds.

145
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:15,900
But we can't pass, we can't just blow past the main characters of the story.

146
00:11:15,900 --> 00:11:23,540
The first officially recorded announcement of the birth of the Messiah was giving to shepherds in a field outside of town.

147
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:31,400
Many of us, we've heard this story so many times that it's just become normal to us, but nothing could be further from the truth.

148
00:11:31,400 --> 00:11:41,360
There's nothing normal about the most important announcement ever in human history given to lowly shepherds out in the fields,

149
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:44,760
unless you know the God of the Bible.

150
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:51,100
Again, this is arguably the most important proclamation in all of human history,

151
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:56,040
and it was entrusted to the least likely cast of characters by any worldly standards.

152
00:11:56,040 --> 00:11:57,660
Most shepherds were very young.

153
00:11:57,660 --> 00:12:03,560
They were members of a poor working class with not much social status or influence to speak of.

154
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:09,340
And again, by worldly standards, this makes no sense at all.

155
00:12:09,340 --> 00:12:15,940
But if you know the God of the Bible, this fits his overarching redemptive narrative from the beginning of the story to the end of the story.

156
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:21,340
Abraham, Moses, David, all names that most of you, even if you didn't grow up in church, know,

157
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:30,200
they were all lowly shepherds in a field before God grabbed them and invited them into his plan for human history.

158
00:12:30,200 --> 00:12:40,280
My point here is this, that the God of the Bible is in the business of using the ordinary to do the extraordinary by his power and his grace.

159
00:12:40,280 --> 00:12:46,720
So if you're in here and you feel ordinary or insignificant, just know that you are the perfect candidate for the kingdom of God.

160
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:54,220
God is in the business of entrusting what seems weak and foolish to the world to bring about his kingdom purposes.

161
00:12:54,220 --> 00:13:00,380
These are the main characters of the most important announcement in human history.

162
00:13:02,620 --> 00:13:08,000
Now, let's look at their response when an angel shows up unexpected in the middle of night.

163
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:17,660
Now, although shepherds may not have much social status to speak of, they are tough individuals, not easily scared.

164
00:13:17,780 --> 00:13:22,600
They spend their lives protecting their flock from outside intruders.

165
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:32,840
Like this is their life, being comfortable in the dark, waiting for surprise attacks from anyone that might want to feed on their flock.

166
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:34,580
They're not easily frightened.

167
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:44,440
But when it comes to the messenger of God showing up and yielding the glory of God, the visible, tangible expression of God's holiness, his set-apartness, they weren't just afraid.

168
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:46,600
They were terribly afraid.

169
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:55,040
And again, even though these are tough individuals, it's not shocking to me that when God's presence shows up to them that they're afraid.

170
00:13:55,040 --> 00:13:59,100
This is the typical, appropriate response up to this point in the Bible.

171
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:05,660
Whenever God's presence shows up to a person in the Bible to this point in human history, everyone is afraid.

172
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:08,280
This is typical.

173
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:09,000
It's normal.

174
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:16,360
Whenever the people in the biblical narrative, humanity encounters the defined, we see this time and time again.

175
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:20,520
But I should make the note that this was not God's original design.

176
00:14:21,940 --> 00:14:28,500
The first pages of Scripture show God walking in the garden with Adam and Eve.

177
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:39,220
This is God's original design, that there was not fear, that there was not worry, anxiety, that there was comfort and peace walking with the king of the universe in the garden.

178
00:14:39,220 --> 00:14:43,560
But we made a decision to try and elevate ourselves to the position of God.

179
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:49,460
And when we made that decision, it became abundantly, invisibly clear that we are not like him.

180
00:14:49,460 --> 00:14:53,520
That he is totally, utterly different than us.

181
00:14:53,520 --> 00:15:01,180
When we tried to elevate ourselves to a God-like status, the great divide between humanity and God became utterly, invisibly clear.

182
00:15:01,180 --> 00:15:03,140
1 Timothy 6 puts it this way.

183
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:08,740
God lives in light so brilliant that no human can approach him.

184
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:20,660
And so you can see how the angels might be sore afraid when the angel shows up with a visible, tangible expression of God's glory.

185
00:15:20,660 --> 00:15:23,700
So they're terrified.

186
00:15:23,700 --> 00:15:27,060
But the angel is very quick to calm their fears.

187
00:15:27,060 --> 00:15:29,840
He immediately says, do not be afraid.

188
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:31,420
Why?

189
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:39,600
Because he has good news that will result in great joy and will be available for all people.

190
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:48,140
Even though their response was normal and expected, the angel says, don't, don't be afraid.

191
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:51,040
I have good news to share with you.

192
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:53,320
Things are about to change.

193
00:15:53,440 --> 00:16:03,980
Contained within this very concise and yet incredible announcement, in my opinion, is the key to understanding how we can be a people that embody authentic internal joy.

194
00:16:03,980 --> 00:16:08,680
This third way, this joy that transcends the circumstances of our life.

195
00:16:08,680 --> 00:16:13,280
We can learn about how to have that in the midst of this announcement from the angel.

196
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:20,360
Again, even though this is the typical and appropriate response, the angel says, resist the temptation of fear.

197
00:16:20,360 --> 00:16:34,520
What's contained in the good news that will lead to great joy is this idea that the God who has been previously hidden behind a thick curtain, relatively inaccessible in the temple, has drawn near, so near.

198
00:16:34,520 --> 00:16:48,380
See, don't be afraid, the God who dwells in unapproachable light, the God who you should be afraid of, is about to change some things.

199
00:16:48,380 --> 00:16:51,340
He has stepped into our world.

200
00:16:51,340 --> 00:16:54,480
He has taken on human flesh.

201
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:55,840
He has drawn near to us.

202
00:16:55,840 --> 00:17:00,500
The God whom we are afraid to draw near to has, in love, drawn near to us.

203
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:05,900
We have to resist the temptation to normalize this story because it's familiar to us.

204
00:17:05,900 --> 00:17:11,040
The God who dwells in unapproachable light took on flesh and dwelt among us.

205
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:17,020
And this is what the angel calls good news.

206
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:23,520
Good news that will result in great joy and be accessible for everyone.

207
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:30,160
Now, sometimes I avoid doing this, but because I'm wearing glasses this morning, let's talk about Greek.

208
00:17:31,280 --> 00:17:36,700
The Greek word here for good news, we've talked about this a lot.

209
00:17:36,700 --> 00:17:37,920
It is euangelion.

210
00:17:37,920 --> 00:17:40,380
It is where we get the word gospel.

211
00:17:40,380 --> 00:17:46,900
We've talked about this a lot in the last couple of months, so I'm not going to belabor an old point, but this is where we get the word gospel.

212
00:17:46,900 --> 00:17:47,940
And we've talked about this.

213
00:17:47,940 --> 00:17:54,500
Unfortunately, in the American church specifically, this word has come to primarily mean the message about where we go when we die.

214
00:17:55,220 --> 00:18:01,140
And although that is included in the gospel, it is nowhere near the central point of the gospel.

215
00:18:01,140 --> 00:18:08,240
In the historical context, even the shepherds with no social status would have been familiar with this word.

216
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:11,260
They would have known what this word means in the context.

217
00:18:12,040 --> 00:18:17,500
This word was very simple and contextual and relatable in the culture.

218
00:18:17,500 --> 00:18:30,600
When a king would have victory over their enemies or would conquer a new territory, they would send an evangelist with a euangelion, with an announcement of good news.

219
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:33,580
They would proclaim the good news of their victory.

220
00:18:33,580 --> 00:18:35,580
One king defeating another king.

221
00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:42,080
And this is the exact format that Luke uses when he's writing his letter, when he's writing his gospel.

222
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:43,600
This is not an accident.

223
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:48,900
He is using the very format of a kingdom proclamation.

224
00:18:48,900 --> 00:19:00,320
God sends a messenger to earth proclaiming gospel, good news that will result in great joy for anyone who has ears to hear.

225
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:02,400
So what's the news?

226
00:19:03,680 --> 00:19:14,400
Well, it follows that example of a king sending a messenger to tell the good news that a kingdom has been defeated or will be defeated today in the city of David.

227
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:17,800
The promised Messiah has been born, God in the flesh.

228
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:22,140
He will save people from their sins.

229
00:19:22,140 --> 00:19:27,380
This is the good news that leads to great joy.

230
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:34,340
This is the first gospel proclamation from heaven to earth after Christ was born.

231
00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:44,860
God, the king of heaven, is proclaiming through this messenger that a new kind of king has been born and a new kind of kingdom is being established.

232
00:19:44,860 --> 00:19:48,100
The one true king came from heaven to earth.

233
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:55,760
He is the one who will have victory over the kingdoms of this world, over the dark spiritual forces and principalities, over all evil and injustice.

234
00:19:55,760 --> 00:19:59,240
A king has come and a kingdom is being established.

235
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:11,600
And anyone who has ears to hear can join and participate in this new kingdom that will be victorious over all injustices, over all sin and destruction that we have let into this world.

236
00:20:14,800 --> 00:20:20,920
Sacrificial love will conquer death and will remove any obstacle in the way of humanity and the divine.

237
00:20:20,920 --> 00:20:31,200
Anyone who has ears to hear this message is invited to God's kingdom to receive great joy that isn't dependent on earthly circumstances.

238
00:20:33,180 --> 00:20:35,900
Now, I know what some of you might be thinking.

239
00:20:35,900 --> 00:20:40,900
Ryan, you sound an awful lot like fake Frank right about now.

240
00:20:40,900 --> 00:20:51,560
So, okay, what you're saying here is just forget your problems because God has come near and he wins in the end.

241
00:20:53,120 --> 00:20:54,420
A little bit like fake Frank, right?

242
00:20:54,420 --> 00:20:57,860
Was I the only one thinking it when I wrote my notes?

243
00:20:57,860 --> 00:20:59,240
I was writing my notes.

244
00:20:59,240 --> 00:21:00,160
I was like, oh, fake.

245
00:21:00,160 --> 00:21:00,860
That's not bad.

246
00:21:00,860 --> 00:21:01,160
All right.

247
00:21:01,160 --> 00:21:01,900
No.

248
00:21:01,900 --> 00:21:11,220
Let's move towards a working explanation of how to embody this biblical joy and get practical so that we're not just like fake Frank.

249
00:21:11,220 --> 00:21:12,160
God wins in the end.

250
00:21:12,160 --> 00:21:12,920
Everything's great.

251
00:21:12,920 --> 00:21:14,140
I'm not going to worry about anything.

252
00:21:15,620 --> 00:21:21,700
In order to do this, I want to show a connection between the first two words of the angel's announcement.

253
00:21:21,700 --> 00:21:23,320
We're going to do Greek twice today.

254
00:21:23,320 --> 00:21:25,340
It's the glasses.

255
00:21:25,340 --> 00:21:26,160
It's the glasses.

256
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:32,200
Okay, so we said euangelion, good news, the gospel.

257
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:43,260
Now, if we were to take the good news, the gospel, and we were to shrink it down and put one word as the defining attribute to the gospel of Jesus,

258
00:21:44,540 --> 00:21:46,420
I'm nervous.

259
00:21:46,420 --> 00:21:56,060
I'm thinking about inviting you to give me your guesses, but if somebody gives a guess that's better than the one that I have in my notes, I'm going to look foolish.

260
00:21:56,060 --> 00:22:01,820
So, I'm just going to tell you my opinion, and then if you disagree with me, tell me after service, okay?

261
00:22:01,820 --> 00:22:08,520
A lot of words could be used to be the central descriptor of the gospel of Jesus.

262
00:22:10,220 --> 00:22:15,840
My working theory is that the most central adjective for the gospel is grace.

263
00:22:17,380 --> 00:22:20,320
Grace is God's undeserved kindness.

264
00:22:20,320 --> 00:22:25,520
Psalm 103 says it this way, the Lord is compassionate and merciful.

265
00:22:25,520 --> 00:22:29,540
This is a echo back to God giving his name to the people of Israel.

266
00:22:31,320 --> 00:22:32,860
God is compassionate and merciful.

267
00:22:32,860 --> 00:22:35,640
He's slow to get angry and filled with unfailing love.

268
00:22:35,640 --> 00:22:39,440
He will not constantly accuse us nor remain angry at us forever.

269
00:22:39,440 --> 00:22:41,620
He does not punish us for all of our sins.

270
00:22:41,620 --> 00:22:45,020
He does not deal harshly with us as we deserve.

271
00:22:45,020 --> 00:22:46,380
This is the gospel.

272
00:22:46,620 --> 00:22:54,120
We deserve judgment and punishment, and instead, God extends his grace, his undeserved kindness and forgiveness to us.

273
00:22:54,120 --> 00:23:01,680
For his unfailing love towards those who fear him is as great as the height of the heavens above the earth.

274
00:23:01,680 --> 00:23:09,060
Grace, the central descriptor of the gospel of Jesus, the good news, is charis.

275
00:23:10,560 --> 00:23:13,540
Now, the next word in the announcement is joy.

276
00:23:13,540 --> 00:23:17,100
Take a look at grace now.

277
00:23:17,100 --> 00:23:19,360
Does it look familiar?

278
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:21,300
Look similar?

279
00:23:21,300 --> 00:23:23,820
Charis, chara.

280
00:23:23,820 --> 00:23:33,660
My point here is that biblical joy that is not shaken by external circumstances is rooted in the grace of God.

281
00:23:33,660 --> 00:23:34,500
I'd put it this way.

282
00:23:34,500 --> 00:23:35,320
This is my main point.

283
00:23:35,400 --> 00:23:39,060
If you're a note taker, I didn't put notes in the bulletin, I'm so sorry, but here's the main point.

284
00:23:39,060 --> 00:23:40,660
Wait, I did.

285
00:23:40,660 --> 00:23:41,760
I did.

286
00:23:41,760 --> 00:23:43,600
I wasn't going to, and then I changed it.

287
00:23:43,600 --> 00:23:44,440
Okay, it's down there.

288
00:23:44,440 --> 00:23:44,740
Perfect.

289
00:23:44,740 --> 00:23:45,600
All right, here it is.

290
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:55,740
For you note takers, your capacity for joy is directly connected to the amount of God's grace and truth that you choose to acknowledge and receive.

291
00:23:55,740 --> 00:24:02,980
Joy is directly connected to the amount of grace and truth that we choose to acknowledge and receive.

292
00:24:03,040 --> 00:24:07,220
We're going to dig into that a little bit because it is a broad concept.

293
00:24:07,220 --> 00:24:13,600
Biblical joy that isn't taken away by our circumstances, by the seasons that we're experiencing.

294
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:21,120
I heard it put this way, that joy is not based on the seasons we're experiencing, but the soil that we are planted in.

295
00:24:21,120 --> 00:24:34,680
Joy is rooted in our capacity to acknowledge and receive God's undeserved kindness to us, his forgiveness, his mercy, his grace.

296
00:24:36,040 --> 00:24:40,440
And we can get as practical or as spiritual as you want to get here this morning.

297
00:24:40,440 --> 00:24:47,800
And this is why I've heard pastors in the past talk about only Christians know what true joy really is.

298
00:24:48,440 --> 00:25:01,760
And although I understand the sentiment, I think that's a little bit off because even people who don't acknowledge God's grace in their life still receive joy from the grace that God is extending to them.

299
00:25:02,740 --> 00:25:17,820
Right, like beautiful, healthy relationships, a good meal, breath in our lungs, the view from an incredible sunset, the emotional response of a powerful song, money in the bank, a healthy body and mind.

300
00:25:17,820 --> 00:25:21,780
All of these things are undeserved kindness from God to people.

301
00:25:21,880 --> 00:25:30,040
And whether or not they acknowledge that it's a gift from God or not, they receive it as a gift and express joy for those things.

302
00:25:30,040 --> 00:25:42,300
The problem with that being the cap of the grace that we're willing to receive from God is all of those things can be taken away in an instant.

303
00:25:42,300 --> 00:25:49,880
Beautiful sunsets only last for a few minutes.

304
00:25:51,880 --> 00:25:55,820
Powerful songs fade into the distance.

305
00:25:55,820 --> 00:26:02,860
Our favorite meal turns into, wow, I ate way too much and now I feel miserable.

306
00:26:02,860 --> 00:26:08,120
Right, like all of these things, breath in our lungs, a healthy body and mind.

307
00:26:08,120 --> 00:26:13,660
All of us have been surprised by an unexpected diagnosis of ourselves or someone else.

308
00:26:13,760 --> 00:26:28,500
And if our joy is rooted in those temporary circumstances, our joy is rooted in our status in society, the job that we have, all of these things can be taken away in an instant.

309
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:36,540
The joy that God invites us to, the great joy that cannot be taken away by circumstantial evidence goes beyond that.

310
00:26:36,540 --> 00:26:37,600
It starts with that.

311
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:40,280
We should acknowledge the good gifts of God in our life.

312
00:26:40,280 --> 00:26:42,920
The practical, joyful gifts.

313
00:26:42,920 --> 00:26:45,420
And some of us have more of those things than others.

314
00:26:48,920 --> 00:26:49,860
Envy, anybody?

315
00:26:49,860 --> 00:26:59,660
Anybody lose sight of the good things that God has been giving to you, the goodness of God in your life because you're too busy looking at the person down the street who has more than you?

316
00:26:59,660 --> 00:27:07,060
Joy starts with our capacity to acknowledge the good practical gifts that God has given us.

317
00:27:07,060 --> 00:27:13,980
Even if we're in our worst season of life, what small, practical gifts from God can you dwell on?

318
00:27:13,980 --> 00:27:16,840
Can you remind yourself that God didn't have to give me this?

319
00:27:16,840 --> 00:27:19,040
He treats me as I don't deserve.

320
00:27:19,040 --> 00:27:22,200
He doesn't extend the wrath and justice that I deserve.

321
00:27:22,200 --> 00:27:25,100
Instead, he extends grace and patience and love.

322
00:27:25,100 --> 00:27:28,280
What practical things can we acknowledge?

323
00:27:28,280 --> 00:27:32,060
But then the believer goes deeper than that.

324
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:43,340
We say not only that, but if we're a follower of Jesus, we know that God promises even our worst circumstances, our worst experiences of life will be redeemed for good.

325
00:27:43,340 --> 00:27:47,620
I said this a couple weeks ago, this line that's been resonating with me.

326
00:27:47,620 --> 00:27:49,900
If it's not good, God's not done.

327
00:27:49,900 --> 00:27:55,840
That doesn't mean that God caused or even we theologize this stuff a lot.

328
00:27:56,920 --> 00:28:03,360
And anytime that something bad happens to us, we either say, we don't want to say that God caused it, but we say that God allowed it.

329
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:08,940
Sometimes bad things happen to us because we are in a broken world full of sinful people.

330
00:28:08,940 --> 00:28:12,900
Sometimes sinful people do bad things to you.

331
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:27,280
And yet, in the midst of that, God says that he will redeem those negative experiences for his good and for his glory and your good for those who love him and are called according to his purposes.

332
00:28:27,280 --> 00:28:39,700
Our joy goes a little bit deeper because even in the worst experiences of our life, we can have confidence that God is somehow going to redeem that for good.

333
00:28:40,060 --> 00:28:41,500
Might take a long time.

334
00:28:41,500 --> 00:28:52,440
We might not see it for a long time, but this is the promise that God takes the bad, negative, worst experiences of our life and he will redeem them for good.

335
00:28:52,440 --> 00:28:54,580
We can have joy.

336
00:28:54,580 --> 00:29:03,340
Not joy that denies the bad experiences, not joy that explains it away, but joy that says, I'm going to stay rooted in God's grace here.

337
00:29:03,340 --> 00:29:07,620
Because I have gifts that he didn't have to give me.

338
00:29:08,120 --> 00:29:09,620
I've got breath in my lungs.

339
00:29:09,620 --> 00:29:18,040
Not only that, but he's somehow, some way, I don't know it, but I've seen him do it in other people's lives and I've read about it in the Scriptures.

340
00:29:18,040 --> 00:29:23,960
So I believe that he is somehow, in some way, going to work these negative experiences in my life for good.

341
00:29:23,960 --> 00:29:25,820
Somehow I'm going to trust him in that.

342
00:29:25,820 --> 00:29:27,120
I'm going to wait for him in that.

343
00:29:27,120 --> 00:29:32,720
And not only that, it goes a layer deeper to our eschatological hope, our end times hope.

344
00:29:32,720 --> 00:29:33,660
Again, that was the glasses.

345
00:29:33,660 --> 00:29:34,180
I'm sorry.

346
00:29:34,500 --> 00:29:36,600
It's making me say words that I wouldn't normally say.

347
00:29:36,600 --> 00:29:39,860
Our future hope, sorry.

348
00:29:39,860 --> 00:29:40,540
I don't know why.

349
00:29:40,540 --> 00:29:41,560
All right.

350
00:29:41,560 --> 00:29:53,320
If God's temporary goodness to us wasn't enough, if his willingness to work out our negative experiences ultimately for good isn't enough,

351
00:29:54,180 --> 00:29:55,820
we have a future hope.

352
00:29:55,820 --> 00:30:02,880
Our temporary struggles are nothing to be compared to the future grace that God has promised us.

353
00:30:04,260 --> 00:30:14,360
And so our biblical joy that isn't taken away by external circumstances is how much grace we're willing to acknowledge and receive from God.

354
00:30:15,420 --> 00:30:18,760
Pastor Michelle showed me this illustration years ago.

355
00:30:18,760 --> 00:30:21,660
And anytime I talk about joy, I think I'm going to use it because I love it.

356
00:30:21,660 --> 00:30:23,240
We've got two balloons here.

357
00:30:23,240 --> 00:30:24,960
Everybody see the balloons?

358
00:30:24,960 --> 00:30:31,300
I watch, Merce and I have been watching a lot of, is this a safe space for confession?

359
00:30:31,300 --> 00:30:37,460
Merce and I have been watching a lot of Hallmark the last couple days.

360
00:30:39,460 --> 00:30:42,680
Hey, I love Hallmark 10 on a lot of holidays.

361
00:30:42,680 --> 00:30:44,140
I'm not even going to pretend like I don't.

362
00:30:44,140 --> 00:30:50,060
But what I don't love is for every six minute of movie they show, they show seven minutes of advertisements.

363
00:30:50,060 --> 00:30:58,800
And some of those advertisements are like the ShamWow guy for like three minutes trying to tell you about this thing that he's going to,

364
00:30:58,800 --> 00:31:01,160
it's going to revolutionize your kitty litter.

365
00:31:02,120 --> 00:31:08,580
And the reason, that is not in the notes.

366
00:31:08,580 --> 00:31:15,480
I bring that up because they're like, these guys are so good at selling you something that I know for a fact isn't going to work.

367
00:31:15,480 --> 00:31:20,000
And so then I stand up here with my two balloons and I'm like, look at the balloons.

368
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:23,020
I'm like, why can't I have that gift?

369
00:31:23,020 --> 00:31:25,460
Why can't I have that energy, that passion?

370
00:31:25,460 --> 00:31:27,900
Because they're selling you something that's not going to work.

371
00:31:27,900 --> 00:31:30,060
And I believe I'm selling you something that's going to work.

372
00:31:30,220 --> 00:31:33,640
And yet I just don't have it in me to try and give you the infomercial voice.

373
00:31:33,640 --> 00:31:36,540
So, but wait, there's more.

374
00:31:36,540 --> 00:31:40,080
So I've got two balloons up here.

375
00:31:40,080 --> 00:31:43,780
One filled with air that represents joy.

376
00:31:43,780 --> 00:31:47,500
These are the, this is the joy that is rooted in the temporary grace of God.

377
00:31:47,500 --> 00:31:50,140
The good gifts that God gives us.

378
00:31:50,140 --> 00:31:51,120
We can have joy.

379
00:31:51,120 --> 00:31:52,660
I picked the yellow balloon.

380
00:31:52,660 --> 00:31:53,840
Joy, right?

381
00:31:53,840 --> 00:31:56,540
Bright, happy, exciting.

382
00:31:56,540 --> 00:31:59,820
We can have joy based on the temporary goodness of God.

383
00:31:59,820 --> 00:32:04,980
But what happens when we face trial, difficulty, circumstances change.

384
00:32:04,980 --> 00:32:07,740
The things that we rooted our joy in are taken away.

385
00:32:07,740 --> 00:32:09,820
Everybody, trigger warning.

386
00:32:09,820 --> 00:32:18,640
It didn't take that long when I tested it.

387
00:32:18,640 --> 00:32:19,840
I was nervous that it wasn't going to work.

388
00:32:19,840 --> 00:32:22,760
Our joy can be taken from us in an instant.

389
00:32:24,140 --> 00:32:26,740
One change in our life circumstances.

390
00:32:26,740 --> 00:32:31,700
And if our, if our joy was rooted in those temporary circumstances, it can all be taken away.

391
00:32:31,700 --> 00:32:39,720
But a joy that is rooted in the grace and truth of God is filled with the living water of God.

392
00:32:39,860 --> 00:32:45,460
John 4 verse 14 says, but those who drink the water I give will never be thirsty again.

393
00:32:45,460 --> 00:32:52,020
It becomes a fresh bubbling spring within them, giving them eternal life.

394
00:32:52,820 --> 00:33:02,300
If we take our joy beyond just our temporary circumstances and we believe wholeheartedly that God is going to somehow redeem the negative situations in our life for good.

395
00:33:02,300 --> 00:33:07,300
And not only that, but even if we can't see him redeeming our negative circumstances for good.

396
00:33:07,300 --> 00:33:11,500
We believe that because he was faithful in the past that he will be faithful in the future.

397
00:33:11,500 --> 00:33:13,960
That his promises do remain true.

398
00:33:14,060 --> 00:33:16,000
We can remain rooted in our joy.

399
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:17,400
Oh no, this is a problem.

400
00:33:17,400 --> 00:33:18,680
I popped the candle that I needed.

401
00:33:18,680 --> 00:33:19,620
All right.

402
00:33:19,620 --> 00:33:20,800
We got it.

403
00:33:20,800 --> 00:33:24,600
All right.

404
00:33:24,600 --> 00:33:33,140
What happens to this person whose capacity for joy is rooted in not just the practical temporary gifts of God,

405
00:33:33,140 --> 00:33:35,960
but the spiritual and eternal gifts of God?

406
00:33:35,960 --> 00:33:39,680
You guys nervous?

407
00:33:39,680 --> 00:33:41,920
I'm a little bit.

408
00:33:41,920 --> 00:33:42,460
I'm not.

409
00:33:43,480 --> 00:33:44,520
How are we doing down there?

410
00:33:44,520 --> 00:33:46,580
Do we still have joy?

411
00:33:46,580 --> 00:33:52,180
Joy, joy, joy, joy down in my heart.

412
00:33:52,180 --> 00:33:52,600
Where?

413
00:33:52,600 --> 00:33:53,920
Love it.

414
00:33:53,920 --> 00:33:55,700
Okay.

415
00:33:55,700 --> 00:33:59,700
Our joy is able to remain, but this is where I want to transition from.

416
00:33:59,700 --> 00:34:04,400
The capacity of joy is directly connected to the amount of God's grace that we receive.

417
00:34:04,400 --> 00:34:12,900
And I want to transition to truth because what we're going to see here with this balloon is that just because our joy remains doesn't mean we don't face

418
00:34:12,900 --> 00:34:16,560
face the negative consequences of the negative consequences of the external experiences of our life.

419
00:34:16,560 --> 00:34:24,340
There are visible, tangible pain that we experience through the difficulties of this life,

420
00:34:24,340 --> 00:34:32,560
which is why joy isn't just rooted in the grace that we receive, but it's rooted in our ability to receive God's truth.

421
00:34:36,560 --> 00:34:42,060
It is right and good to acknowledge that life is not as it should be.

422
00:34:42,060 --> 00:34:45,260
Fake Frank doesn't do anything for anyone.

423
00:34:45,260 --> 00:34:55,900
Fake Frank ignores the present realities of living in this broken world and just tries to fake like God's grace makes everything perfect all the time.

424
00:34:55,960 --> 00:34:56,780
And that's not true.

425
00:34:56,780 --> 00:35:01,940
Unexpected diagnosis has come.

426
00:35:01,940 --> 00:35:09,180
The pregnancy test comes back empty for the fifth, sixth, seventh time.

427
00:35:09,180 --> 00:35:16,040
The person that stood at the altar and vowed to us that they would never betray us.

428
00:35:16,040 --> 00:35:18,720
Betrayed us.

429
00:35:20,080 --> 00:35:21,580
This happens.

430
00:35:21,580 --> 00:35:31,020
And it does nothing for ourselves, for others, or for God to pretend like it doesn't matter because God is gracious to us.

431
00:35:31,020 --> 00:35:37,060
It is right and good to acknowledge that life is not as it should be.

432
00:35:37,060 --> 00:35:41,180
It does not honor God to smile through the pain and pretend like life isn't difficult.

433
00:35:42,120 --> 00:35:43,580
Real people, real lives.

434
00:35:43,580 --> 00:35:49,160
This is one of the things I love the most about our community is that we embody this value most of the time.

435
00:35:49,160 --> 00:35:49,880
We're not perfect.

436
00:35:49,880 --> 00:35:54,060
But I can't tell you the amount of people that I've walked up to in the lobby and said, how are you doing?

437
00:35:54,060 --> 00:35:55,440
And they're like, hey, do you really want to know?

438
00:35:55,440 --> 00:36:00,640
